  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nMid-level water vapor satellite imagery shows a trough over the \nPacNW with a squished ridge just off the OR coast on the heels of \nthe trough. Temperatures warm Saturday as the trough is pushed \neastward and the ridge sets up over the West Coast, increasing \nheights. Even with this pattern change, temperatures will still \nremain below normal by about 1-4 degrees, with highs in the mid 70s \nfor the coast & mountains, 80s for inland spots and High Desert, and \nnear 100 for the lower deserts. The marine layer both Saturday and \nSunday morning will be slightly shallower than its been the past few \ndays, burning off quicker and not making it as far inland. \n\nThe ridge won't remain in place for very long as a secondary trough \nimmediately behind it punts it eastward. This next trough is poised \nto become a closed low, settling over Central CA by Monday. Onshore \nflow will increase late Sunday and Monday while the marine layer \nwill deepen once again. Temperatures in turn will be cooler Sun-Tue, \ncoldest Monday, with highs 5-12 degrees below normal. \n\nThe pattern changes once again Wednesday as the trough becomes \nabsorbed into the upper level pattern with a massive low sitting off \nthe Canadian coastline. High pressure slides over the SoCal waters \nand builds quickly late next week. This should return us to a \nwarming trend, rebounding temperatures back above normal. At this \npoint, models disagree on the overall placement of the high, as well \nas how the aforementioned Canadian low might move. As such, models \nshow a significant temperature spread for the end of next week and \ninto the weekend, with some resolving highs closer to near normal \nwith others around 5-8 degrees above. Some guidance also indicates \nthat the southwesterly flow could push monsoonal moisture back up \ninto the SoCal mountains, but a clearer picture of where the high \npressure may be is needed before there is significant confidence in \nthis.",
